WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
The process creation API shapes the safety properties of the launch.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.280
Some APIs pass a single command string through a shell.

3
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:12.860
Others execute a specific program with a structured argument vector.

4
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:24.640
Some platforms provide spawn-style APIs, lower-level fork-and-exec workflows, or dedicated process creation calls with explicit settings for environment, handles, and working directory.

5
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:29.640
Direct program execution is often easier to reason about when shell features are not required.

6
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:36.140
The program name and each argument remain separate values, so validation can be field-specific.

7
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:43.840
A filename, mode, count, or identifier can be checked as data instead of being merged into one command string.

8
00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:48.760
Shell-based execution adds another parser and another set of quoting rules.

9
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:53.740
That may be necessary for a narrow class of tasks, but it should be justified and reviewed.

10
00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:03.920
If the program does not need shell syntax, redirection, pipelines, globbing, or built-in shell behavior, direct execution is usually the simpler contract.

11
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.880
Platform behavior matters.

12
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:16.060
Argument splitting, environment representation, handle inheritance, process groups, working directory handling, and error reporting vary across systems.

13
00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:22.960
A wrapper function can be useful, but it should expose the decisions that matter instead of hiding them behind a vague command string.

14
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:27.320
The safest API is the simplest one that matches the actual need.

15
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:40.380
Choose the API that lets the code name the program explicitly, pass arguments structurally, control inherited state, check launch errors, and manage the child process lifecycle predictably.
